CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 9 ----------
("node-9", "Node 9 · Physical memory", "What is the relocation problem?", "A compiler bakes absolute addresses into machine code, but the loader does not know where in physical memory the process will sit. Static relocation rewrites every address at load time (slow, and the copy cannot then be moved or shared); dynamic relocation adds a hardware base register to every address, so the program is compiled as if starting at zero."),
("node-9", "Node 9 · Physical memory", "What is external fragmentation, with a concrete example?", "Free memory exists but not in one contiguous piece. Load processes of 100, 300, 200 and 400 MB in a 1 GB machine, exit the second and fourth: 700 MB is free in pieces of 300 and 400, and a 500 MB process cannot start."),
("node-9", "Node 9 · Physical memory", "Why is compaction not the answer to fragmentation?", "It means copying gigabytes while every affected process is stopped. Allocation policies (first-fit, best-fit, worst-fit) reduce but never eliminate the problem."),
("node-9", "Node 9 · Physical memory", "What were overlays and why do they matter to this chain?", "Programmers manually splitting a program into pieces and writing code to load one over another, ensuring no two mutually-calling pieces were swapped out together. It was a hated part of the job into the 1970s and is the reason virtual memory was pursued so hard."),
("node-9", "Node 9 · Physical memory", "State the five requirements Node 9's failures impose on any memory design.", "Translate program addresses to physical ones in hardware; check permissions on every access; place memory non-contiguously so fragmentation cannot block; let a process exceed physical memory without programmer effort; and let two processes share the same physical memory."),
# ---------- Node 10 ----------
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "State the paging idea in one sentence, and say which of Node 9's failures it kills.", "Cut the address space into fixed-size pages and physical memory into frames of the same size, and let any page map to any frame. All five: fragmentation dies, protection is per-page, sharing is two tables pointing at one frame, and a page can be marked absent and fetched on demand."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why 4096 bytes?", "A balance: smaller pages waste less on the final partial page but need far more table entries; larger waste more and make transfers coarser. The VAX chose it in 1977 and it has survived a factor of a million in memory sizes, mostly because changing it would break too much."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "How is a virtual address translated, and why is it not a search?", "The address is cut in two. With 4 KB pages the low 12 bits are the offset within the page, passed through untouched; the upper bits index the table. Translation is an array index and a concatenation — gate delays, not a search."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why is the x86-64 page table four levels of nine bits?", "Nine bits indexes 512 entries; 512 entries of 8 bytes is exactly 4096 bytes — one page. Every level of the tree is itself one page. 48 bits of address = 9+9+9+9 plus a 12-bit offset."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "What are the two facts that together constitute memory isolation?", "Changing <code>cr3</code> changes the meaning of every address in the machine, and only the kernel can execute that instruction."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Name the page table entry flags and what each enables.", "Present (is it in memory — if not the rest of the entry is free for the kernel's own bookkeeping); Writable (clearing it on a shared page gives copy-on-write); User/supervisor (how kernel memory is mapped everywhere yet unreachable); Accessed and Dirty (set by hardware, read by the kernel to choose evictions and writebacks); NX (non-executable, added by AMD in 2003, which is why shellcode on the stack no longer works and why a JIT needs special pages)."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why does a four-level table not cost four extra memory accesses?", "The TLB caches recent translations — 64 to 1536 entries, fully associative — with hit rates well above 99% on ordinary code, because programs touch the same pages repeatedly."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "What problem do huge pages solve, and why do databases often disable transparent huge pages?", "A large randomly-accessed working set misses the TLB constantly; one 2 MB page covers 512 times as much memory per entry, so a 32 GB Postgres buffer pool needs vastly fewer. But <em>transparent</em> huge pages defragment in the background, causing latency spikes — which is why MongoDB and Redis tell you to turn THP off."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "List the six situations a single page fault can represent.", "Valid but not in memory (read it in — a major fault); valid and in memory but not mapped here (a minor fault); first touch of freshly allocated memory (find and zero a frame); a write to a copy-on-write page; growth just below the stack; or genuinely not part of this process — <code>SIGSEGV</code>."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why is it significant that all six are the same hardware event?", "Lazy allocation, demand loading, copy-on-write, growable stacks, memory-mapped files and segmentation faults are one mechanism distinguished only by kernel bookkeeping. It is the most elegant thing in the operating system."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "How do you distinguish major from minor faults, and what does a rising major count mean?", "<code>ps -o min_flt,maj_flt</code> or <code>pidstat -r</code>. Minor faults in the millions are normal and cheap. Major faults climbing in steady state means the machine is fetching pages from disk to run — thrashing — and it will show as latency no application profiler can explain."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Define VSZ, RSS and PSS, and say which one matters.", "VSZ is total mapped address space — what the process asked for, including regions never touched. RSS is how many of those pages are actually resident — what it is using, and what a container limit and the OOM killer count. PSS divides shared pages among their sharers, so it is the honest number when summing across a process tree."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why is a Node process's VSZ often several gigabytes, and should you care?", "V8 reserves large regions of address space it may never populate, and address space is free on 64 bits. No — RSS is the number that consumes a resource."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "What is ASLR and what does it cost you when debugging?", "Randomising the addresses of stack, heap, libraries and the executable on every run so an attacker cannot predict where to jump. It is why native crash addresses differ every time and why symbolising a core dump requires knowing load addresses; <code>randomize_va_space</code> turns it off for debugging."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why does V8 use pointer compression, and what does it cap?", "Object references are stored as 32-bit offsets from a base: half the memory for pointers, and twice as many pointers per cache line so pointer-chasing misses half as often. It caps a single V8 heap at 4 GB of compressed address space — the real reason <code>--max-old-space-size</code> above ~4096 does nothing useful."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "What is a memory-mapped file and name three places it appears in your stack.", "Mapping a file's contents into the address space so reading it is reading memory, with pages faulting in on demand and shared through the page cache. SQLite and LMDB use it as their primary access method; V8 maps its startup snapshot (which is why Node starts in tens of milliseconds); and twenty containers from one image share the executable's text pages."),
("node-10", "Node 10 · Virtual memory", "Why is a <code>Float64Array</code> of a million numbers so much faster to loop over than a million <code>{x,y}</code> objects?", "Eight megabytes of contiguous, prefetchable memory versus a million pointers to a million scattered allocations, each access a potential cache miss at ~80 ns. Factors of five to twenty on numeric loops, and it is the memory hierarchy, not V8 being clever or stupid. It is also why columnar databases exist."),
# ---------- Node 11 ----------
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Sketch a process's address space from high to low.", "Kernel space (mapped everywhere, supervisor-only); the stack, growing down; an unmapped gap; the mmap region (shared libraries, large allocations, mapped files, thread stacks); another gap; the heap, growing up; <code>.bss</code>, <code>.data</code>, <code>.rodata</code>, <code>.text</code>; and page zero, deliberately unmapped."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why does dereferencing null produce a fault rather than reading garbage?", "Page zero is deliberately left unmapped. The safety of null is a page-table decision."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What is W^X, and what must a JIT do about it?", "No region is both writable and executable, enforced by the NX bit — which stops classical shellcode injection. V8 must break it: allocate pages, write machine code, flip them to executable, keeping the window narrow. Where it is forbidden entirely (iOS outside Safari, hardened kernels), JavaScript engines fall back to an interpreter and run several times slower."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why do the stack and the heap grow toward each other?", "A convention from when a process had one contiguous region: putting them at opposite ends let either grow into the shared middle. The unmapped gaps mean overgrowth faults instead of silently overwriting a neighbour."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What is a stack frame and what is a stack trace?", "A frame holds one active call's locals, its extra arguments, and the return address; calling pushes, returning pops, and <code>rsp</code> points at the top. A stack trace is that chain walked."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why do you see <code>RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded</code> rather than a segmentation fault?", "V8 imposes its own smaller limit (~1 MB) and checks it on function entry so it can throw a catchable JavaScript error before the hardware guard page is reached. Hence ~10–12,000 frames rather than a hundred thousand, varying with locals per frame."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why is raising <code>--stack-size</code> past the OS stack a bad trade?", "It turns a catchable <code>RangeError</code> into a hard segmentation fault. Deep recursion in JavaScript is a design problem — engines still do not implement proper tail calls outside Safari — so the fix is an explicit stack, a queue, or iteration."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What are the only two ways a program gets more memory?", "<code>brk</code>/<code>sbrk</code>, which moves a single heap-top pointer (so memory returns only from the top), and <code>mmap</code>, which requests a region of address space with stated properties and is what everything modern uses."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Name the four kinds of <code>mmap</code> and one use of each.", "Anonymous private (plain memory — a large malloc, a JavaScript heap); file-backed private (an executable's data section, copy-on-write); file-backed shared (mmap I/O and shared memory between unrelated processes); anonymous shared (state shared between a process and its children, and <code>/dev/shm</code>)."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why is <code>MAP_ANONYMOUS</code> memory guaranteed zero-filled?", "Security, not convenience: without it a fresh allocation could contain another process's freed data. The kernel keeps a supply of pre-zeroed pages."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What will you see in <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/maps</code> for a running Node process?", "The <code>node</code> binary's <code>r-xp</code> text and its data; an anonymous <code>[heap]</code>; many large anonymous regions that are V8's spaces and the allocator's arenas; mapped shared libraries including any <code>.node</code> addons; one stack per thread; and <code>[vdso]</code> and <code>[vvar]</code>."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Name V8's heap spaces and what each is for.", "New space (young generation, 1–8 MB per semi-space, allocation by pointer bump, collected by copying survivors to the other half — sub-millisecond); old space (promoted objects, mark-sweep-compact, mostly concurrent since Orinoco); large object space (over ~128 KB, never moved); code space (executable JIT output)."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What is the generational hypothesis and what does it imply for your code?", "Most objects die young, so collection cost scales with <em>surviving</em> data rather than allocated data. Ten thousand short-lived objects per request cost almost nothing; ten objects per request added to a module-level cache cost increasingly much, forever."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Read the five numbers from <code>process.memoryUsage()</code>.", "<code>heapTotal</code> and <code>heapUsed</code> are V8's spaces only; <code>external</code> is memory held by C++ objects tied to JavaScript ones, including buffers; <code>arrayBuffers</code> is the <code>ArrayBuffer</code> subset; <code>rss</code> is the operating system's number — all of the above plus the binary, libraries, thread stacks and allocator overhead."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "What is the signature of a buffer leak, and why is it hunted in the wrong place?", "Rising <code>rss</code> and <code>external</code> with a flat <code>heapUsed</code>. Most Node leak tooling looks at the heap, where these bytes do not appear, and <code>--max-old-space-size</code> cannot protect you because the limit only governs old space."),
("node-11", "Node 11 · The address space", "Why must <code>--max-old-space-size</code> be meaningfully below the container limit?", "Because RSS exceeds old space by hundreds of megabytes of binary, libraries, code space, stacks, buffers and fragmentation, and the kernel kills on RSS."),
# ---------- Node 12 ----------
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Is <code>malloc</code> a system call?", "No. It is ordinary unprivileged library code inside your process that obtains large regions with <code>mmap</code> or <code>brk</code> and carves them up — which is the fact that explains most of this node."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why does writing before the start of an allocated block corrupt the heap?", "The allocator stores a small header immediately before the returned pointer recording the block's size. Overwriting it breaks bookkeeping, and the crash appears much later somewhere unrelated."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Describe glibc's allocator in three points.", "Free blocks kept in bins by size so finding a fit is not a linear scan; requests above <code>MMAP_THRESHOLD</code> (128 KB, dynamically adjusted) get their own <code>mmap</code> and are returned to the kernel exactly when freed; and each thread gets its own arena — capped at eight times the core count — so threads do not contend on one lock."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why does <code>free()</code> usually not reduce RSS?", "It returns the block to the allocator's own free lists inside your process, so the next <code>malloc</code> can reuse it without a system call. Correct behaviour, and it means process memory is a high-water mark rather than a current measurement."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Give two reasons memory cannot be returned even when the allocator wants to.", "The heap shrinks only from the top, so one small live allocation near the top pins everything below it; and a page can only be returned if <em>every</em> object on it is free, so a page holding one live 32-byte object stays fully resident."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Account for the gap between <code>heapUsed</code> of 200 MB and a container using 800 MB.", "V8's <code>heapTotal</code> headroom; external memory (buffers, ArrayBuffers, addon allocations); the <code>node</code> binary and shared libraries (50–100 MB resident); V8's code space; thread stacks; and glibc arena fragmentation — often the largest surprise on many-core machines."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "What does <code>MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2</code> do and when is it worth setting?", "Caps the number of glibc arenas, trading a little allocation contention for substantially less resident memory. It is one of the few genuinely useful environment variables for containerised Node and Java services with unexplained RSS."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why do people <code>LD_PRELOAD</code> jemalloc?", "jemalloc and tcmalloc are designed around fragmentation avoidance and multi-threaded scalability, and swapping glibc's allocator without recompiling routinely reduces RSS by twenty to forty percent on allocation-heavy workloads. Sometimes 'our service uses too much memory' has a one-line answer involving no code."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "State the three layers of allocation.", "The kernel hands out pages via <code>mmap</code>; the allocator carves those into objects; the garbage collector decides which objects are reachable and returns the rest to layer two — which usually does not return them to layer one."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why is 'unreferenced' the wrong model for garbage collection?", "Collection traces reachability from roots — the global object, the call stack, handles held by native code. Anything unmarked is garbage. This is why tracing can collect a cycle and pure reference counting cannot."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Name five ways a JavaScript memory leak actually happens.", "A module-level Map or array growing per request; an event listener added per connection and never removed, retaining its whole closure; a <code>setInterval</code> closing over a large object; a promise that never settles, retaining its continuation and captured scope; and V8's shared closure context, where an unrelated small closure keeps a large captured variable alive."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "How do GC pauses show up, and what actually fixes them?", "A scavenge is sub-millisecond; a full mark-compact of a 2 GB old space is tens to low hundreds of milliseconds, mostly concurrent but with a real compaction stop. It appears as p99 spikes uncorrelated with request complexity; find it with <code>--trace-gc</code> or a <code>PerformanceObserver</code> on <code>gc</code> entries. The fixes are architectural — allocate less per request, keep the live set small, split large heaps across processes."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Derive a heap cap for a 1 GB container, and say what happens if you set none.", "Roughly 60–75% — 640 to 768 MB — then measure. With no cap, V8 derives its default heap size from the <em>host's</em> physical memory, so on a 64 GB node a 512 MB container gets a V8 that will not collect urgently until well past the point the kernel kills it."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why does <code>work_mem</code> in Postgres multiply dangerously?", "It is per sort or hash operation, <em>per plan node, per concurrent query</em> — so a modest-looking value can multiply by a hundred under load and exhaust the machine. It is the Postgres version of setting no heap cap."),
("node-12", "Node 12 · The allocator", "Why does a native addon leak produce a flat heap graph and no GC activity?", "It calls <code>malloc</code> directly, so V8 does not know that memory exists, does not count it towards the heap limit, and feels no pressure from it. <code>napi_adjust_external_memory</code> exists so well-behaved addons can tell V8; many do not."),
# ---------- Node 13 ----------
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "What is demand paging and what does it explain about cold starts?", "The kernel does not read a program into memory; it marks pages absent, records where they live, and jumps to the entry point, faulting pages in as they are touched. It is why a program starts in milliseconds regardless of size, and why the first request to a fresh process is slower — the code paths it exercises are being faulted in."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "What is the page cache, and why is low free memory healthy?", "All otherwise-unused memory is used to cache file pages; every ordinary read and write goes through it. Free memory is doing nothing, cached memory saves disk reads and is instantly reclaimable. Read <code>available</code>, not <code>free</code>."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "What happens on a <code>write()</code> to a file, before any <code>fsync</code>?", "Your bytes are copied into a cache page, the page is marked dirty, and the call returns. No disk has moved. Kernel threads write back later, governed by <code>vm.dirty_ratio</code>, <code>dirty_background_ratio</code> and <code>dirty_expire_centisecs</code>."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "How does the kernel approximate LRU cheaply, and what does the active/inactive split prevent?", "Hardware sets the accessed bit on any access; the kernel periodically clears and re-reads them — the clock or second-chance algorithm. Two lists mean a page must be referenced while inactive to be promoted, which stops one large sequential read from evicting the whole useful working set (cache pollution — why a nightly backup used to slow a database for an hour)."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "What is a working set and what is thrashing?", "Denning, 1968: the set of pages a process has touched recently, a good predictor of what it will touch next. If the sum of all working sets exceeds physical memory, every fault evicts a page another process is about to need — the machine spends all its time moving pages and none doing work, and it collapses rather than degrading."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why do file-backed pages not need swap but anonymous pages do?", "A file-backed page's home is the file, so it can always be dropped. Heap, stack and anonymous mappings have nowhere to go without a dedicated swap area — without swap they can never be evicted at all."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why did Kubernetes historically refuse to run with swap enabled?", "Swap makes memory limits unenforceable and latency unpredictable — a page from RAM is 80 ns, from an SSD ~100 µs, a thousand times slower — and the scheduler's model assumes memory is memory. Support returned gradually from 1.28 onward, but for latency-sensitive services the default position is still no swap."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why does Linux overcommit memory?", "It has to in practice: <code>fork</code> plus copy-on-write doubles address space that will almost never be realised, and programs reserve large sparse regions they never touch — V8 among them. <code>vm.overcommit_memory</code> controls the policy."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why does the OOM killer use <code>SIGKILL</code> and what does that cost you?", "A page fault on legitimately-granted memory with no frame available has no sensible failure path — <code>mov</code> has no error return. So the kernel kills the highest scorer outright. <code>SIGKILL</code> cannot be caught, so there is no flush, no close, no draining, and no log line."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Decode exit codes 137, 143 and 139.", "A process killed by a signal reports 128 + the signal number. 137 = 128+9 = <code>SIGKILL</code> (in a container, almost always the cgroup OOM killer). 143 = 128+15 = <code>SIGTERM</code>, a normal shutdown. 139 = 128+11 = <code>SIGSEGV</code>."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why can a container be OOMKilled on a machine with plenty of free memory?", "The cgroup memory limit is a separate, per-container OOM domain. It exhausted its own budget, not the machine's."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "How can a container with no memory leak at all be OOMKilled?", "Page cache from the container's own file writes is charged to its cgroup. Under memory pressure with heavy write throughput — logs, a temp file, SQLite, an upload buffer — it can be killed with anonymous usage far below the limit. Symptom: OOMKilled with a flat heap; evidence: the anon/file split in <code>memory.stat</code>."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why is choosing which limit you hit first a design decision?", "Hitting V8's heap limit throws a catchable fatal error with a stack trace and can trigger <code>--heapsnapshot-near-heap-limit</code> — diagnosable. Hitting the kernel's limit produces one line in the host's kernel log and nothing else."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Why does Postgres deliberately keep <code>shared_buffers</code> modest?", "It expects the operating system's page cache to hold the rest, accepting double buffering because the kernel's cache and readahead are good. <code>effective_cache_size</code> is not an allocation but a planner hint about total caching — set too low, it makes the planner avoid index scans it should choose. It is also why a tight container memory limit hurts Postgres in a way more <code>shared_buffers</code> does not fix."),
("node-13", "Node 13 · Paging pressure", "Explain a serverless cold start in terms of this node.", "The first invocation faults in the runtime, the dependency tree and JIT-compiled code page by page from storage. Hence bundle size affecting cold start more than steady-state performance, and hence snapshot-based fixes (Lambda SnapStart, V8 snapshots, <code>--snapshot-blob</code>) attacking it by having the pages already laid out."),
# ---------- Node 14 ----------
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "How does a process know whether it is the parent or the child after <code>fork()</code>?", "The call returns twice: the child's PID in the parent (or −1 on failure) and 0 in the child. That is the only asymmetry in the whole call."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "Describe copy-on-write step by step.", "The child gets its own page table pointing at the <em>same</em> physical frames; every writable page is marked read-only in both and flagged COW; reads are free and fully shared; a write raises a protection fault; the kernel allocates a fresh frame, copies 4 KB, points the writer's entry at it, marks both writable, and re-executes the instruction."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "What does forking a 2 GB process actually cost?", "The page tables and nothing else — a millisecond or two. If the child immediately <code>exec</code>s, essentially zero pages are ever copied. This is why overcommit is a practical necessity, not just a permissive default."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "What is <code>clone()</code> and why is it the most clarifying call in Linux?", "One system call whose flags say exactly what the new task shares — address space, descriptors, signal handlers, namespaces. <code>fork</code> shares almost nothing; a thread shares almost everything; a container adds namespace flags. Threads, processes and containers are one system call with different arguments."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "Why can Redis memory nearly double during <code>BGSAVE</code>?", "It <code>fork</code>s to get a frozen consistent view, which is nearly free — <em>if the parent is not writing</em>. Under heavy writes the parent dirties pages and each is copied. It is also why Redis explicitly recommends <code>vm.overcommit_memory=1</code>: with the default heuristic the fork itself can fail."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "Why can you not safely <code>fork()</code> a Node process?", "<code>fork</code> duplicates only the calling thread, but every lock the other threads held stays held forever by threads that no longer exist. If a libuv or V8 thread held the allocator lock, the child deadlocks on its first allocation. POSIX permits only async-signal-safe functions between fork and exec, and <code>malloc</code> is not one."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "What do <code>cluster.fork()</code> and <code>child_process.fork()</code> actually do?", "Spawn a brand-new <code>node</code> process via <code>posix_spawn</code> and <code>execve</code>, with an IPC channel attached. They are not the <code>fork()</code> system call — the name is inherited vocabulary, and confusing the two is a common interview stumble."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "Name five places copy-on-write appears above the kernel.", "Docker image layers (copy-up on first write, at file granularity); filesystem and cloud disk snapshots (block granularity); Postgres MVCC (a new row version rather than an overwrite — hence VACUUM); Git's immutable content-addressed objects (hence free branching); and React/Immutable structural sharing (new objects only along the path to the change — hence identity comparison as a change check)."),
("node-14", "Node 14 · fork and COW", "When you meet a system claiming cheap snapshots or cheap branching, what should you ask?", "What it costs when things do get written. That is almost always copy-on-write, and the write path is where the cost was deferred to."),
]
